# Summary of changes for run 6c31efea-75d9-4c65-a3ec-7eb894202a5d
I wrote and published the five Phase B files. The article and paper are self-contained, with no mention of Lean, proof assistants, verification, file paths or formal identifiers. Each theorem is stated in ordinary mathematical prose with a proof sketch.

- **`ARTICLE.md`** (about 2,200 words) is the popular-science piece. It starts with the quadratic sieve as a race between rows and columns, then introduces split and inert primes through the Legendre symbol, the hit count \(1+(N/r)\), and the hope that "large primes" would supply columns outside the split set. It then gives the one-line refutation (\(r \mid c^2-N \Rightarrow N\equiv c^2 \pmod r\)) and works through the instance \(N=15916539906629\), \(c=3989570\). Two further points: reciprocity means large primes can't be cherry-picked, and the large primes cancel after pairing, so the final matrix still has \(Q(B)\) columns.
- **`RESEARCH_PAPER.md`** (about 4,400 words) and **`RESEARCH_PAPER.tex`** (amsart, mirrors the Markdown) contain the abstract, definitions and full statements with proof sketches. The results covered are:
  - large primes are split, with the Euler form;
  - inert primes never divide a value of \(c^2-N\);
  - the hit count is \(1+(N/r)\);
  - the multiple-polynomial version for discriminant \(D\);
  - the support and column-containment theorems for any number of large primes;
  - a single large prime gives \(Q(B)<Q(L)\), and \(Q(L)=Q(B)+\#\)(split primes in \((B,L]\));
  - pigeonhole pairing, and the combined-relation theorem (\(Q(B)+1\) rows suffice, so the final width doesn't depend on \(L\));
  - splitness depends only on the class of \(r\) mod \(4N\).

  The paper also has a numerical section, three algorithms, a discussion and four conjectures. It states plainly that the partial-relation stage adds split columns while the final matrix keeps \(Q(B)\) columns, so the large-prime variant only adds rows; it neither decouples nor enlarges the final column count.
- **`demo.py`** uses only the standard library and runs in about 1 second. It shows:
  - the headline instance: \(128878271\) is prime and its symbol is \(+1\);
  - a scan of 400 values: all 996 odd prime divisors are split, none inert;
  - hit counts for odd primes below 60;
  - the classes mod 60 for \(N=15\);
  - two complete toy large-prime factorisations, including \(1000000016000000063 = 1000000007\times1000000009\) with an 857 × 277 final matrix;
  - a simulation of the conjectured birthday law, clearly labelled as conjectural.
- **`PACKAGE.json`** has every schema field filled in: metadata (domain "Novelty", 5 key results, 8 keywords), the full article, paper, LaTeX and demo text, 1 demo, 3 algorithms (each with pseudocode and code), 2 matplotlib visualizations, 2 interactive HTML widgets, and a guided interactive layout with collapsible proofs and external links. It also includes the Phase A Lean source (`Catalog/Novelty/LargePrimeSplitLegendre.lean`) and the Phase A future directions.

